  "detail": null,
  "summary": "I reviewed all 7 patients on the 'DOAC dose review - week of 10/19/2026' list under RBC-PHARM-07. Five ended on a new dose, two kept their dose, and every change has a signed progress note with its sources cited.\n\n- pt-0061 Castellano: apixaban 2.5 → 5 mg BID (mr-t0001). She meets none of the dose criteria (age 74, 78 kg, SCr 1.02), and her itraconazole course ended 08/14 according to the ID letter.\n- pt-0003 Delgado: no net change, rivaroxaban 20 mg daily (mr-t0011). I first lowered him to 15 mg based on an outside creatinine of 160 µmol/L. St. Brigid then corrected it to 118 µmol/L (1.34 mg/dL), which gives CrCl 60, so I put him back on 20 mg and wrote a correction note.\n- pt-0041 Duquette: apixaban 5 → 2.5 mg BID (mr-t0003). He meets no criteria, but he has an active itraconazole order. I left the itraconazole alone, as the policy requires.\n- pt-0062 Fairweather: apixaban 5 → 2.5 mg BID (mr-t0013). This was correct on the clinic labs, but a new St. Brigid creatinine of 150 µmol/L (1.70 mg/dL) arrived during the review. With age 82, that makes two criteria.\n- pt-0038 Moravec: rivaroxaban 15 → 20 mg daily (mr-t0004). The earlier pharmacist screen used ideal body weight. Using his actual weight (96 kg, SCr 1.45, age 79), CrCl is 56. He then sent a portal message with a home scale weight of 185 lb. The policy doesn't count a self-reported weight, so the dose stays and I documented it. That weight (about 84 kg) would put his CrCl near 49, under the 50 cutoff for 15 mg, so a clinic-measured weight at his next visit would settle it.\n- pt-0043 Pellegrino: rivaroxaban 20 → 15 mg daily (mr-t0014). Her 10/07 labs supported 20 mg, but a new creatinine of 1.25 on 10/22 gives CrCl 44.\n- pt-0002 Vance: no net change, apixaban 2.5 mg BID (mr-t0012). He meets only one criterion (age 82; 64.9 kg, SCr 1.21), so I first raised him to 5 mg. A St. Brigid dermatology letter then arrived showing he started itraconazole on 10/22, so I set him back to 2.5 mg and wrote a correction note.\n\nTwo patients (Duquette, Vance) are on the lower apixaban dose only because of itraconazole. Their doses should be looked at again when the 12-week courses end: mid-December for Duquette and mid-January 2027 for Vance. I didn't create follow-up tasks for this.\n\nI completed the 5 inbox items that arrived during the review. Nothing else new came in. I didn't touch the inbox items that were already there and aren't related to this list.",
